<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/409-1865-dakota-territory-map-cropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Created by order of President Abraham Lincoln in 1861, Dakota Territory was formed from all or part of what are now the states of North Dakota, South Dakota, Montana, and the northern half of Wyoming. At that time, the territory was occupied by a handful of trappers and fur traders living among nine separate Native American tribal nations. New York City newspapers in 1837 touted the railroads as "the swift civilizer of the wilderness." Reflecting the prevalent attitude of the time, which defined civilization as a European-derived culture, this early desire for transcontinental railroads was driven primarily by commerce. But how would construction be financed? At a public meeting on January 4, 1847, a New Yorker proposed funding railroad projects by selling land adjacent to the railroad to emigrants. A mob accosted him, claiming the idea was a swindle planned by conspirators to rob the government of its lands. Yet a variation of this scheme was eventually used to fund such projects.</p>

<p>1871</p>

<p>In July 1871, the Northern Pacific Railroad began construction of a northern transcontinental rail line between Duluth and the west coast.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/708-key-track-completion-dates.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Thomas Rosser, chief engineer of the Northern Pacific Railroad’s Eastern Division, retraced and evaluated the 1854 proposed route between the Missouri River near Mandan and the Yellowstone River near today's Glendive, Montana. His group traveled with a large military escort out of Fort Rice in the fall of 1871. They returned by steamboat without incident.</p>

<p>1872</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/334-orders-65-1872-april-cropped.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>The U.S. Army issued Special Orders No. 65 on April 16, 1872, to establish an encampment and supply depot supporting construction of a Northern Pacific Railroad bridge across the Missouri River. No specific location was identified. An oral history refers to the site as "Camp Green," coincidentally sharing the name of the officer assigned to the mission. Some local residents claim the camp was to be established along the Missouri River near the Little Heart River. However, in June 1872, the U.S. Army began constructing an infantry post designated Fort McKeen above the confluence of the Heart and Missouri rivers, near the former site of a fur-trading post.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/327-1876-map.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>This fort was located 30 miles north of the existing Fort Rice, approximately 5 miles south of what would eventually become Mandan. Consistent with the siting and design criteria used by the U.S. Army at the time, the site was established on top of river bluffs and surrounded with a wood stockade complete with blockhouses. Its location was far more defensible. Construction of Fort McKeen began in June by companies B and C of the 6th Infantry under the command of Lt. Col. Daniel Huston Jr.</p>

<p>The fort was located near the former Mandan settlement that has come to be known as On-A-Slant Village. Estimated to have included about 75 earthlodges and perhaps 1,000 residents, the village had likely existed for over 200 years before a 1781 smallpox epidemic decimated the population. Construction of a wooden bridge across the Red River of the North was completed and the railroad came to Fargo in June 1872. Frederic Gerard first settled on the future Mandan townsite. General D. S. Stanley and his troops based out of Fort Rice again accompanied now two railroad survey crews to scout a new route west beyond the confluence of Glendive Creek and the Yellowstone River. Several battles with the Lakota involving Gall and Sitting Bull occurred. Lives were lost on both sides including one of the two railroad survey crews. The expedition returned to Fort Rice in mid-October. On November 20, 1872, Fort McKeen was renamed Fort Abraham Lincoln.</p>

<p>1873</p>

<p>A new county named "Morton" was created on January 8, 1873, by the territorial legislature. However, administrative positions remained vacant and documentation remained incomplete. Although the county organization was not completed at that time, the new county was not attached to any other county for administrative or judicial matters. Northern Pacific Railroad construction progressed relatively unimpeded until it reached the Missouri River at Bismarck in June. The Port of Bismarck supplied the upper Missouri River region, transferring freight from rail warehouses to steamships serving military and civilian settlements as far as Fort Benton, Montana Territory.</p>

<p>In March 1873 Congress authorized the addition of a cavalry post to Fort Abraham Lincoln when foot soldiers were deemed ineffective against their mounted adversaries. By the fall of 1873, the 7th Cavalry and the 17th Infantry joined the 6th Infantry, increasing the fort’s population from 137 men and officers to 655. To accommodate this increase, 700 carloads of supplies and materials were shipped to Dakota Territory to construct seven officers’ quarters, a granary, office and dispensary, guard house, commissary storehouse, quartermaster’s storehouse, three soldiers’ barracks with attached mess room and an ordnance depot. A civilian settlement named "Lincoln" sprang up just beyond the walls of the cavalry fort.</p>

<p>The third railway survey expedition left Fort Rice in July with 373 men and 275 supply wagons bound for the Yellowstone, with General Stanley in command. The group proceeded through the area of present-day Glendive, Montana, and continued to Pompey's Pillar. Lt. Colonel Custer commanded the 7th Cavalry accompanying the expedition. Six violent encounters suggest that members of the area's Native American tribes had concluded the surveying had gone far enough. During one fight, Custer had his horse shot out from under him. The expedition returned to Fort Lincoln and Fort Rice in September. In November, George Custer and his wife, Libbie, moved to Fort Abraham Lincoln, where Custer assumed his role as post commander.</p>

<p>1874</p>

<p>1878</p>

<p>The earliest homesteaders and land speculators arrived and settled within what would become the city limits of Mandan. Henry Helmsworth, described as the first white baby born in the Mandan area, was born on June 14, 1878, to Mr. and Mrs. Alex Helmsworth, Sr. Their home stood north of the railroad viaduct on Memorial Highway, near today’s intersection of Main Street and Mandan Avenue. Mrs. Sarah Boley attended the birth. (Mandan had not yet been officially incorporated as a village.) Morton County completed its administrative organization on November 5, 1878. Lincoln, the civilian settlement adjacent to Fort Abraham Lincoln and home to support staff such as interpreters, was named the county seat. As reported in the <em>Bismarck Tribune</em>, a railroad crew led by Thomas Rosser surprised the town in December 1878 when it arrived to begin construction.</p>

<p>1879</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/319-1879-us-dept-of-interior-cushman-enlargement.jpg" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p> With the start of Northern Pacific Railway's bridge across the Missouri and the corresponding surge of activity on the west bank, citizens of Burleigh County from the east bank successfully lobbied the 1879 territorial legislature to redraw Morton County's boundary to include the west bank communities of Mandan (formerly Morton, formerly Cushman), Lincoln, and Fort Abraham Lincoln. The move boosted Burleigh County's reported population in the official 1880 census by 69%, enhancing Bismarck's efforts to win as the new location for the capital of Dakota Territory in 1883.</p>

<p>The city's first newspaper, the <em>Mandan Criterion</em>, began publication on May 24, 1879. Harry Robinson, originally from Wisconsin, arrived in town on May 16, opened his print shop, and published the weekly newspaper until at least June 1887. The first citywide Fourth of July celebration included a rodeo, foot races for children and adults, pony races, horse races, wheelbarrow races, music, and dancing at the "rustic dance hall."</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1870s/doc-pdf.gif" alt="Historic image from the Coming of the Railroad 1870-1879 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>A wide range of businesses were established in the city and began advertising their services in the local newspaper.</p>

